\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models ($\varepsilon$-free; $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ applies to selection only, stable across $[0, 0.2]$, Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon_robustness}).

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC (medium difficulty), structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (single-sample F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, macro F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

Two mechanisms block selection: instance degeneracy collapses the candidate pool for consensus-based approaches, and weak LP-quality alignment prevents exploiting the remaining headroom.
Four instance-level signals cannot rank candidates by construction; LP tends to succeed when both low degeneracy and adequate alignment ($\rho \gtrsim 0.3$, approximate boundary from our data) are present.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ NER selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. Best non-oracle in \textbf{bold}. Non-tied subset in \S\ref{sec:discussion}; RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.775 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Per-sample signal}} \\
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Instance-level signals (consensus-proximity selection)}} \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & --- \\
\midrule
Random           & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Despite moderate quality estimation, no signal consistently improves selection over greedy (Table~\ref{tab:selection}).
Instance-level signals assign identical QE scores to all candidates; MBR conversion does not improve over greedy on the primary test bed.

\paragraph{No significant improvement over greedy.}
On SciERC ($n{=}529$, 2-seed sign tests\footnote{MDE${\approx}$1.5~pp at 80\% power ($n{=}529$); Few-NERD ($n{=}32{,}565$; MDE${\approx}$0.22~pp) provides the definitive test.}), the oracle (0.775) reveals 13.0~pp of headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$~pp ($p{=}0.29$), below the MDE (${\approx}$1.5~pp).
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}); under stringent TOST anchors, some configurations show significant \textit{degradation} (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
Few-NERD provides the definitive contrast: LP achieves $+1.39$~pp ($p{<}0.001$) at low degeneracy (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}), while structural signals still degrade selection.
Instance-level signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} is best; closing this gap likely requires trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers} (the sole positive finding, non-tied LP subset $+2.9$~pp $p{=}0.004$, survives Bonferroni correction; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}
\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered). Instance degeneracy (Degen.\%): fraction where all $N$ samples yield identical F1. $p$: paired bootstrap ($n{=}10{,}000$) vs.\ greedy. Few-NERD and CoNLL $\Delta$ values are 3-seed means; SciERC is single-seed (3-seed mean: $+0.04{\pm}0.48$~pp). Oracle headroom: 4.0~pp (CoNLL), 13.0~pp (SciERC), 12.4~pp (Few-NERD). Minor greedy F1 variations across tables reflect protocol differences (\S\ref{sec:evaluation}). Few-NERD uses 3-epoch checkpoints; SciERC and CoNLL use 5 epochs (Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 48.4\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .644 & 17.8\% & .205 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.8\% & .421 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms that LP selection effectiveness is inversely associated with degeneracy.
Under standard early stopping (3 epochs for Few-NERD vs.\ 5 for SciERC/CoNLL), Few-NERD degeneracy is 13.8\%, and LP achieves $+1.39$~pp ($p{<}0.001$, 3-seed mean ${\pm}0.04$~pp), while all structural signals degrade selection (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp).
Training convergence modulates degeneracy: a 5-epoch Few-NERD control raises degeneracy to 31\% and halves LP gains, though selection remains positive (Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

\paragraph{Empirical pattern for LP selection.}
The cross-dataset gradient is consistent with an inverse relationship (48.4\% $\to$ $-0.85$~pp, 17.8\% $\to$ $+0.61$~pp, 13.8\% $\to$ $+1.39$~pp; $n{=}3$ datasets, exploratory given 5+ confounds; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), with supporting evidence from per-entity-type analysis within Few-NERD ($n{=}8$, $\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$; entity types share model, tokenizer, and temperature, so effective degrees of freedom may be lower than 6).
Low degeneracy alone does not ensure selection gains: the LoRA-rank ablation (\S\ref{sec:robustness_main}) reduces degeneracy to 7.2\% yet LP does not improve selection because $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){\approx}0.24$.
Few-NERD exhibits both favorable factors: low degeneracy (13.8\%) and strong alignment ($\rho{\approx}0.5$; per-type detail in Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).
All five signals achieve positive QE on Few-NERD (LP $\rho{=}0.42$, VC $0.39$), yet only LP translates QE into selection; the gap confirms that strong instance-level QE cannot rank candidates within an instance.
Conditional analysis on the favorable subset (non-degenerate instances whose within-instance LP range exceeds the dataset-specific median) yields $+2.47$~pp on SciERC (CI $[+0.30, +4.70]$) and a marginal positive trend on Few-NERD ($+0.63$~pp, CI $[-0.02, +1.30]$); CoNLL remains negative ($-1.78$~pp), consistent with ceiling-regime degeneracy even within the favorable subset (Appendix~\ref{app:conditional_decomp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain. Blue circles: cross-dataset ($n{=}3$, exploratory). Orange triangles: per-entity-type within Few-NERD ($n{=}8$, $\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$; types share model and tokenizer). The dashed line at ${\sim}$15\%\protect\footnotemark{} separates regimes where LP improves (left) vs.\ shows insufficient evidence (right).}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

\footnotetext{Boundary between Person (13.4\%, $+1.25$~pp) and Art (19.8\%, $-0.17$~pp).}

\paragraph{LP selection scales with sample budget.}
Doubling $N$ from 8 to 16 on Few-NERD yields $+0.52$~pp (paired $p{=}0.003$, CI $[+0.17, +0.87]$), driven by reduced degeneracy (14.6\% $\to$ 7.0\%) expanding headroom (12.1 $\to$ 15.4~pp), while LP ranking quality remains stable ($\rho{\approx}0.62$).
Structural signals remain uniformly negative at $N{=}16$; the gap persists regardless of sample budget.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); LP shows the largest temperature sensitivity ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.044$).
Signal rankings are stable across seeds (Kendall's $W{=}1.0$ at $N{=}8$; $m{=}3$ raters, interpret with caution given low $m$; exact $p{\approx}0.028$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}).
NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: Qwen3-8B vs.\ 4B NER quality estimation ($\rho$; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed${=}42$). 8B values identical to Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}; 4B uses matched evaluation protocol. RE scale ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll cc r@{}}
\toprule
& & \textbf{8B} & \textbf{4B} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & SJ & .436 & .422 & $-$.014 \\
      & FK & .400 & .418 & $+$.018 \\
SciERC & SJ & .360 & .422 & $+$.062 \\
       & FK & .267 & .326 & $+$.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
With Qwen3-4B (Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation}), QE is comparable (mean $|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$), yet selection remains below greedy despite 13.0~pp headroom. Task difficulty is a stronger predictor than model scale within the 3B--8B range (Appendix~\ref{app:llama3b}).
Degeneracy scales with capability: 8B shows 17.8\% vs.\ 4B's 10.4\% on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).

\paragraph{LoRA-rank ablation.}
Training with LoRA rank${=}8$ (vs.\ 32) lowers degeneracy from 17.8\% to 7.2\% and increases headroom to 16.0~pp, but LP does not improve selection (0.635 vs.\ greedy 0.640).
This ablation conflates diversity with model capacity (rank 8 has fewer trainable parameters); within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){\approx}0.24$ remains too weak despite wider LP spread (0.057 vs.\ 0.041 nats).
Temperature manipulation (\S\ref{sec:discussion}) provides a cleaner test by altering diversity without changing capacity, and yields the same conclusion: both low degeneracy and adequate alignment appear independently associated with selection success.

\paragraph{Supervised signal combination.}
Supervised classifiers (logistic regression, MLP, random forest) trained on all five signals (5-fold CV, 3-seed mean, SciERC, $N{=}16$, $+17.9$~pp headroom) all perform at or below greedy (F1${=}0.644$; LR: $-0.4$~pp; MLP: $-1.6$~pp; RF: $-3.1$~pp).
Adding pairwise interaction features does not help (AUROC 0.634 vs.\ 0.638).
To test whether richer representations could close the gap, we train a DeBERTa-v3-base verifier that encodes input-output pairs and predicts per-sample F1.
DeBERTa achieves $\rho{=}0.329{\pm}0.087$ (5-fold CV, 3 seeds). Despite moderate quality prediction, selection utility is poor: on SciERC, DeBERTa closes $-26.5{\pm}26.5$\% of the oracle gap (13 of 15 runs negative; vs.\ LP's $+4.2{\pm}10.7$\%); on Few-NERD, DeBERTa recovers only $+3.5$\% (single 80/20 split; vs.\ LP's $+16.3$\%).
Cross-dataset transfer fails entirely ($-27$\% to $-72$\% gap closure).
Neither handcrafted signal combinations (LR, MLP, RF on 5 scalar signals) nor learned text representations close the selection gap; only model-internal log-probability provides meaningful selection signal, consistent with an information asymmetry between the generating model and external observers.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Probing the Information Gap}
\label{sec:hidden_probe}

The preceding negative results suggest the bottleneck is model-internal rather than a feature engineering problem.
We test this directly by training probes on the generating model's hidden representations.

\paragraph{Setup.}
For each of $N{=}8$ samples per instance, we extract the mean-pooled final-layer hidden state ($d{=}4096$) from the generating Qwen3-8B model and train a linear probe and a two-layer MLP (hidden dim${=}256$) to predict per-sample F1 (5-fold CV, 3 seeds).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Hidden state probes vs.\ LP for sample selection (5-fold CV ${\times}$ 3 seeds). $\rho$: Spearman correlation with per-sample F1. Sel.\ F1: selection F1. Gap\%: oracle gap closure. SciERC greedy${=}0.644$, oracle${=}0.775$.}
\label{tab:hidden_probe}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}l ccc cc@{}}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{Few-NERD}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6}
Method & $\rho$ & Sel.\ F1 & Gap\% & $\rho$ & Gap\% \\
\midrule
LP & .283 & $\mathbf{.650}$ & $+$4.8 & .606 & 12.3 \\
Hidden Linear & $.179{\pm}.019$ & $.613{\pm}.002$ & $-$23.4 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{---} \\
Hidden MLP & $\mathbf{.388{\pm}.013}$ & $.637{\pm}.003$ & $-$5.1 & $\mathbf{.629{\pm}.007}$ & 11.7 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Cross-dataset asymmetry.}
On SciERC, the MLP probe achieves higher quality correlation than LP ($\rho{=}0.388$ vs.\ $0.283$, $+37$\%); hidden states encode information beyond what LP captures.
Yet higher correlation does not yield better selection: MLP selection F1 ($0.637$) falls below LP's ($0.650$), with gap closure turning negative ($-5.1$\% vs.\ $+4.8$\%; Table~\ref{tab:hidden_probe}).
The linear probe performs worse still ($\rho{=}0.179$, gap closure $-23.4$\%).
On Few-NERD, the MLP provides no increment over LP (gap closure $11.7$\% vs.\ $12.3$\%).
A learned token-level LP aggregator (1D CNN over per-token log-probabilities) matches mean LP on Few-NERD (gap closure $56.4$\% vs.\ $55.8$\%), confirming that LP's bottleneck is not token aggregation.
The correlation-selection gap thus extends to model-internal representations: in the high-capability regime (greedy F1${=}0.644$), inter-sample F1 differences are small enough that improved ranking provides no marginal selection value, consistent with the capability-diversity tension (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\phantomsection\label{sec:selection_results}
%% Framework validation (DGS) is presented in Discussion §5 to save space

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
RE quality estimation is reported in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed} (3-seed means).
The signal ranking differs from NER: VC leads ($\rho{=}0.309{\pm}0.024$), but LP rises to \#2 ($\rho{=}0.297{\pm}0.022$), above FK, SJ, and EM, a reversal reflecting RE's higher sensitivity to per-sample scoring.
RE miscalibration is more severe than NER (ECE 0.109--0.339; Figure~\ref{fig:reliability}).

\paragraph{Selection.}
Table~\ref{tab:re_cond_selection} presents RE selection at $N{=}8$: no signal outperforms single-sample (0.404) on the full set ($n{=}397$) or the non-zero subset ($n{=}238$), with 18.1~pp of unrealized oracle headroom.
At $N{=}16$ (Qwen3-4B, Table~\ref{tab:re_n16_selection}), LP is the sole positive signal ($\Delta{=}{+}0.7$~pp, $p{=}0.058$), consistent with LP being the strongest RE selection signal across scales.

\paragraph{Scale ablation.}
At 4B scale (Qwen3-4B, $N{=}8$, $\text{seed}{=}42$), RE quality estimation shows FK $\rho{=}0.359$ and LP $\rho{=}0.351$, both above their 8B counterparts (FK 0.275, LP 0.266), while SJ declines ($0.267$ vs.\ $0.250$).
Selection remains below single-sample on RE ($\Delta$ up to $-3.4$~pp) despite 17.1~pp of oracle headroom.

\paragraph{Free-form decoding.}
RE LP selection consistently reverses direction under free-form decoding ($+1.8{\pm}0.1$~pp across 4 seeds vs.\ $-0.9$~pp under constrained decoding at 8B; \S\ref{app:freeform_ablation}); constrained decoding's fixed template likely obscures LP signal in RE (\S\ref{app:mbr_discussion}).

\section{RE Conditional Selection}\label{app:re_conditional_selection}

Table~\ref{tab:re_cond_selection} extends the selection analysis to the RE non-zero F1 subset.
Of $n{=}397$ RE instances, 238 (60\%) have non-zero F1 and 159 (40\%) have zero F1.
All five signals underperform single-sample on both subsets.
On the non-zero subset, selection performance is \textit{worse} than on the full set (e.g., VC: $-10.0$~pp on non-zero vs.\ $-4.5$~pp on full set), because single-sample is already strong on non-zero instances (F1${=}0.674$) and signals actively select inferior samples.
Oracle headroom exists (0.757 vs.\ 0.674) but no signal exploits it, ruling out the 40\% zero-F1 floor effect as the explanation for RE selection failure.

